    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that's helpful. One more thing—since this is a law firm, the client is really worried about the floor looking \"tired\" or damaged after the first year. They're concerned about those tiny, inexplicable scuff marks or small chips that seem to appear out of nowhere in high-traffic spots. Between the three, which one is most likely to show that kind of \"unexplained\" wear and tear over time?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If the client is absolutely terrified of *any* visible wear in the first year, I'd caution them that all materials will show *some* sign of life, but the nature of the wear differs. Natural stone is the most prone to showing subtle signs of wear, like micro-scratches or dulling of the finish from constant friction. While polished concrete is tough, those tiny, persistent scuffs can become very noticeable if the sealant breaks down in high-traffic lanes. LVT is designed to mask minor surface wear better than the other two, making it the least likely to scream \"damage\" from small, unexplained scuffs, provided you use a high-grade commercial product.",
